## Problem

When a task lane ends (failed or timed-out) while its worktree is in the middle of a `git merge`, the WIP candidate commit fails with `fatal: cannot do a partial commit during a merge.` The lane result then has no `target_sha`, so downstream verification skips it and the work only survives as an unresolved merge in the retained worktree.

Observed 2026-09-26 in a multi-lane session: lane `ws-link-guard-e` ended with 46 changed paths and `MERGE_HEAD` present; its result blocker was `failed WIP candidate commit failed: git commit failed: fatal: cannot do a partial commit during a merge.`

A related variant: a timed-out lane with a clean tree reports `WIP candidate commit failed: ... nothing to commit, working tree clean` as a blocker, although the branch tip already holds the lane's commits. The tip should be the candidate.

## Expected

- If `MERGE_HEAD` exists at the end of the lane, then either commit the merge as a WIP merge commit (`git commit` with no pathspec, all conflicts staged) when no conflict markers remain, or report a distinct blocker `unresolved merge in progress` with the conflicted paths. Do not report a generic commit failure.
- If the tree is clean and the branch has commits beyond the base, set `target_sha` to the branch tip instead of reporting a WIP commit failure.

## Why it matters

Both cases lose the candidate sha, so the orchestrator must inspect the worktree by hand. A continuation lane cannot start from a named commit.

